is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: OSPF adjacency to the WAN edge. CSW1’s OSPF process and all internal core adjacencies are healthy; the failure is the OSPF peering from CSW1 toward the WAN router (R1).
- Mechanism: With the CSW1–R1 OSPF neighbor down, no default route is injected into the IGP. Internal routes (between Offices A and B) remain via CSW1/CSW2 to the distribution switches, so inter-office pings still work. Internet-bound traffic fails due to the missing 0.0.0.0/0.
- Localization: On CSW1, OSPF neighbors are Full on bond0 (to CSW2) and on eth3–eth6 (to DSWA1/DSWA2/DSWB1/DSWB2). The only OSPF interface without a neighbor is eth0 (10.0.0.34/30, CD_WAN_LINK_1) toward R1. “show ip ospf interface” on CSW1 shows eth0 as DR with Neighbor Count 0, and “show ip route 0.0.0.0/0” is empty on CSW1/DSW pairs. On R1, “show ip ospf neighbor” returns “ospfd is not running.”
- Root cause: R1’s OSPF daemon is not running, causing the OSPF neighbor on CSW1 eth0 to go down and removing the default route from the core.
- Corrective fix: Start/restore OSPF on R1 and ensure it forms adjacency on CD_WAN_LINK_1 (R1.eth0 10.0.0.33 — CSW1.eth0 10.0.0.34). Advertise the default route into OSPF (default-information originate) so the core regains 0.0.0.0/0. Verify CSW2’s WAN OSPF as well. Note: Inter-office reachability is currently healthy (e.g., PC1 can reach 10.3.0.100 and 10.5.0.4), corroborating that the issue is limited to the WAN default path, not CSW1’s internal uplinks. 
=== END ===